{"anchor":"day at the office","spark":"waking with pain to piss","wound":"fear of news of the fleet","fuel":null,"bridge":null,"echo":"lady sandwich's calm temper","narrative":"He woke before 4 o'clock with great pain in pissing, thinking he had drunk too great a draught of cold drink before bed, but went back to sleep. He was very busy at the office all morning and dined with Sir G. Carteret and the Board, where there was a good pasty and brave discourse. Their great fear was fresh news of the fleet; the report said all was well and the Dutch beaten, but he did not give it much belief, and the simply written news from Sir W. Batten made them all make good mirth. He was vexed that the Controller had done nothing to right the King in Sir G. Carteret's accounts, then wrote letters all afternoon, went by coach about his Tangier business to get money, and visited my Lady Sandwich, who expected every hour to hear of my Lord but was in the best temper he ever saw, neither confident nor fearful. He returned home before 12 o'clock and went to bed.","bridge":null}

