{"anchor":"day at walthamstow","spark":"agreed trip to walthamstow","wound":"not in humour with the tangier demands","fuel":"shared merriment","bridge":null,"echo":"excellent merry discourse","narrative":"After doing business at the office until ten, the narrator went with my lord, Sir J. Minnes and Sir W. Doyly by boat and then Sir W. Batten's coach to Walthamstow, merrily talking about the previous night's revels. They had a plain venison dinner, played billiards where he won an angel, and joked about a pretended warrant to vex Sir W. Hickes, who was out of humour. Full of good victuals and mirth they went home in the evening, then at Greenwich he found Lord Rutherford and Creed with orders for Tangier money, including over L7000 to this lord. He gave the best answer he could, to pay with tallys, was not in humour to spend much time with them, and then went by water to Woolwich, played a game at tables with his wife, and went to bed.","echo_note":null}

